1
00:00:05,449 --> 00:00:13,920
In Dracula, a ship drifts into Whitby harbour in a storm with fifty boxes of earth on board, and Mina Murray reads all about it in the

2
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:14,802
newspaper.

3
00:00:15,134 --> 00:00:18,976
This guide covers the novel and a version where she reads it twice.

4
00:00:20,156 --> 00:00:24,047
Hello, and welcome to Unwritten Chapters, Study Edition.

5
00:00:24,271 --> 00:00:28,331
In the next few minutes, I'll walk you through Dracula, by Bram Stoker.

6
00:00:28,474 --> 00:00:33,025
It's a quick guide you can use for class, a book club, or your own curiosity.

7
00:00:33,216 --> 00:00:37,319
The full guide, with ten museum and archive sources, is linked below.

8
00:00:39,739 --> 00:00:45,231
Dracula is told in diaries, letters, telegrams and newspaper cuttings.

9
00:00:45,651 --> 00:00:53,953
It begins with a young English solicitor's clerk, Jonathan Harker, travelling to Transylvania to help Count Dracula buy a house in

10
00:00:53,953 --> 00:00:56,502
England, an old place called Carfax.

11
00:00:56,854 --> 00:01:00,741
At the castle, Jonathan slowly realises he's a prisoner.

12
00:01:01,417 --> 00:01:05,390
The Count sails to England on a Russian ship called the Demeter.

13
00:01:05,709 --> 00:01:11,490
It crashes onto the beach at Whitby in a storm, with its captain dead and tied to the wheel.

14
00:01:11,818 --> 00:01:16,881
A huge dog leaps ashore, and the cargo is fifty boxes of earth.

15
00:01:17,271 --> 00:01:25,061
Jonathan's fiancée, Mina Murray, is staying in Whitby with her friend Lucy Westenra, who begins to walk in her sleep and fall

16
00:01:25,061 --> 00:01:25,560
ill.

17
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:31,052
Dr John Seward sends for his old teacher, Professor Abraham Van Helsing.

18
00:01:31,208 --> 00:01:38,834
With Lucy's friends, and with Mina's careful notes, they track down the boxes one by one and make them useless to the Count.

19
00:01:38,957 --> 00:01:42,015
In the end they follow him home to Transylvania.

20
00:01:43,533 --> 00:01:45,626
Now for the story.

21
00:01:45,922 --> 00:01:50,117
Our sleep story for this book is called "Mina Keeps Watch."

22
00:01:50,381 --> 00:01:53,406
Here's how it unfolds, chapter by chapter.

23
00:01:54,079 --> 00:01:55,174
Chapter one.

24
00:01:55,343 --> 00:01:56,988
The Storm at Whitby.

25
00:01:57,785 --> 00:02:03,145
Mina Murray is spending the summer in Whitby with her best friend, Lucy Westenra.

26
00:02:03,376 --> 00:02:11,271
Every afternoon they sit on a bench in the churchyard on the East Cliff, below the ruined abbey, and Mina writes in her journal.

27
00:02:11,567 --> 00:02:17,203
She's worried, because her fiancé, Jonathan, has stopped writing from Transylvania.

28
00:02:17,892 --> 00:02:21,963
On the night of the eighth of August, a great storm blows in.

29
00:02:22,225 --> 00:02:28,528
A Russian ship, the Demeter, runs aground below the cliff with her captain dead at the wheel.

30
00:02:28,837 --> 00:02:32,422
A huge dog leaps ashore and vanishes up the cliff.

31
00:02:33,157 --> 00:02:34,292
Chapter two.

32
00:02:34,473 --> 00:02:35,743
The Captain's Log.

33
00:02:36,343 --> 00:02:38,657
The newspaper prints the captain's log.

34
00:02:38,923 --> 00:02:42,643
In the book, Mina finds it sad and turns the page.

35
00:02:42,889 --> 00:02:45,964
Tonight, she reads it twice and makes a list.

36
00:02:46,122 --> 00:02:52,834
The cargo was fifty boxes of earth, sent to a solicitor on the Crescent, a few doors from where she's staying.

37
00:02:53,184 --> 00:03:00,226
She puts on her hat and gloves, visits him, and learns the boxes are going by train to Carfax, near London.

38
00:03:00,553 --> 00:03:05,160
It's the house Jonathan went abroad to sell, right next to Dr Seward's hospital.

39
00:03:05,503 --> 00:03:11,095
Mina writes to Dr Seward, and that night she ties Lucy's door key to her own wrist.

40
00:03:11,632 --> 00:03:12,793
Chapter three.

41
00:03:13,057 --> 00:03:14,406
Fifty Boxes.

42
00:03:14,871 --> 00:03:20,010
Dr Seward sends for his old teacher, Professor Van Helsing, who comes at once.

43
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:26,209
He reads Mina's notes and hangs garlic flowers around Lucy's window, and Lucy sleeps safely.

44
00:03:26,602 --> 00:03:32,011
Then the friends go to Carfax and find all fifty boxes together in the old chapel.

45
00:03:32,345 --> 00:03:35,699
Van Helsing crumbles a little holy bread into each one.

46
00:03:36,059 --> 00:03:39,584
In the book, that's how the earth is made useless to the Count.

47
00:03:39,901 --> 00:03:46,492
Mina checks every box against her list, and when they reach the last one, she draws a neat line under the column.

48
00:03:46,805 --> 00:03:48,853
Fifty. That's all of them.

49
00:03:49,596 --> 00:03:50,945
The Last Page.

50
00:03:51,665 --> 00:03:58,331
The Count arrives to find nowhere to rest, and quietly leaves England on the ship the Czarina Catherine.

51
00:03:58,688 --> 00:04:04,635
Lucy marries Arthur, and Mina goes to Budapest to marry Jonathan, just as she does in the book.

52
00:04:05,123 --> 00:04:10,426
The next summer, they sit together on the bench above Whitby, and Mina shows him her list.

53
00:04:12,178 --> 00:04:12,664
Mina Murray.

54
00:04:12,664 --> 00:04:16,757
An assistant schoolmistress who's learning shorthand.

55
00:04:16,910 --> 00:04:20,055
She notices everything and writes it all down.

56
00:04:20,707 --> 00:04:22,047
Jonathan Harker.

57
00:04:22,307 --> 00:04:27,346
Mina's fiancé, a solicitor's clerk sent to Transylvania on business.

58
00:04:28,127 --> 00:04:29,570
Lucy Westenra.

59
00:04:29,803 --> 00:04:34,474
Mina's best friend, who receives three marriage proposals in one day.

60
00:04:35,184 --> 00:04:36,947
Abraham Van Helsing.

61
00:04:37,184 --> 00:04:42,436
A Dutch professor who knows more about old, strange things than anyone alive.

62
00:04:43,306 --> 00:04:45,025
Dr John Seward.

63
00:04:45,251 --> 00:04:48,921
A young doctor who runs a hospital next door to Carfax.

64
00:04:49,344 --> 00:04:50,639
Count Dracula.

65
00:04:50,845 --> 00:04:56,878
An ancient nobleman from Transylvania who travels to England with boxes of his own soil.

66
00:04:59,078 --> 00:05:00,492
Careful noticing.

67
00:05:00,753 --> 00:05:05,435
Mina wins by reading closely, making lists and checking facts.

68
00:05:05,677 --> 00:05:09,220
Our story shows that patience can be a kind of courage.

69
00:05:09,735 --> 00:05:11,950
Stories inside stories.

70
00:05:12,166 --> 00:05:16,084
The book is a pile of diaries, letters and news cuttings.

71
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:20,752
Each writer knows only part of the truth until the pieces are put together.

72
00:05:21,143 --> 00:05:22,543
Old and new.

73
00:05:22,950 --> 00:05:29,502
The Count is ancient, but his hunters use trains, telegrams, typewriters and shorthand.

74
00:05:29,792 --> 00:05:31,748
Modern tools help them win.

75
00:05:32,566 --> 00:05:33,515
Teamwork.

76
00:05:33,693 --> 00:05:35,883
No one in the book wins alone.

77
00:05:36,097 --> 00:05:38,932
The friends succeed by sharing what they know.

78
00:05:41,096 --> 00:05:42,614
So what's the what-if?

79
00:05:42,722 --> 00:05:44,850
Here's the turning point we changed.

80
00:05:45,534 --> 00:05:51,415
In our story, Mina reads the Demeter's log in the newspaper and doesn't turn the page.

81
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:59,522
She makes a list, visits the Whitby solicitor who took charge of the cargo, and learns that all fifty boxes are going by train to

82
00:05:59,522 --> 00:06:04,830
Carfax, the house Jonathan went abroad to sell, right next to Dr Seward's hospital.

83
00:06:05,298 --> 00:06:11,157
Everything after that moment is new, but the characters still act the way their authors wrote them.

84
00:06:13,057 --> 00:06:16,256
Before we go, here's a little about who wrote it.

85
00:06:17,121 --> 00:06:20,772
Bram Stoker was born in Dublin in 1847.

86
00:06:21,084 --> 00:06:25,937
He was ill for much of his childhood and spent years in bed listening to stories.

87
00:06:26,247 --> 00:06:34,107
As an adult he managed the Lyceum Theatre in London for nearly thirty years, working for the famous actor Henry Irving.

88
00:06:34,203 --> 00:06:39,953
He wrote Dracula in his spare time, after years of reading about folklore and visiting Whitby.

89
00:06:42,092 --> 00:06:45,447
Dracula comes from the end of Queen Victoria's reign.

90
00:06:45,739 --> 00:06:52,486
London had gas lamps, steam trains and telegraphs, and newspapers came out several times a day.

91
00:06:52,778 --> 00:06:59,779
Typewriters and shorthand were new office skills, and many young women like Mina were learning them to find work.

92
00:07:02,121 --> 00:07:03,511
Want to see the real thing?

93
